        DB::table('units')->insert([
            [
                'id' => 4003,
                'title' => 'U4:  Chasing sound dreams',
                'author' => 'Unsung hero',
                'description' => '<p>In this unit, you <strong>will complete</strong> several comprehension exercises about<strong> a family discussion on whether or not to ask a friend for a music audition</strong>. You<strong> will also define</strong> your goals, <strong>identify </strong>persistent comprehension problems and <strong>reflect </strong>on strategies to compensate for them.</p>',
                'listening_tips' => null,
                'cultural_notes' => '<p data-start="147" data-end="546"><strong data-start="147" data-end="169">1. David Smallbone</strong>: He is a real person. An Australian music promoter and the father of several musicians, including Rebecca St. James and Joel and Luke Smallbone (who perform as the Grammy-winning Christian duo for KING &amp; COUNTRY). <strong data-start="384" data-end="392">e.g.</strong> <em data-start="393" data-end="544">David Smallbone has spent his life in the music industry as concert promoter and artist manager, seeking to spread the hope and love in Jesus Christ.</em></p>\\r\\n<p data-start="548" data-end="803"><strong data-start="548" data-end="568">2. Eddie DeGarmo</strong>: He is a real person&mdash;he&rsquo;s a well-known figure in the Christian music industry in the United States. Eddie DeGarmo (born October 3, 1954) is an American contemporary Christian music recording artist, keyboardist, producer and singer.</p>\\r\\n<p data-start="805" data-end="1018"><strong data-start="805" data-end="817">3. gonna</strong> (modal verb): informal for going to. Intending to do something in the future, or being certain or expecting to happen in the future. <strong data-start="951" data-end="959">e.g.</strong> <em data-start="960" data-end="1016">The radio said it was gonna be hot and sunny tomorrow.</em></p>\\r\\n<p data-start="1020" data-end="1164"><strong data-start="1020" data-end="1032">4. gotta</strong> (auxiliary verb): short form of have got to. To need to or be forced to; must. <strong data-start="1112" data-end="1120">e.g.</strong> <em data-start="1121" data-end="1162">We gotta finish this project by Friday.</em></p>\\r\\n<p data-start="1166" data-end="1568"><strong data-start="1166" data-end="1184">5. unsung hero</strong> (idiom): It is generally a compliment. It refers to someone who contributes greatly through their courage, compassion, perseverance, and selflessness without seeking public recognition. <strong data-start="1371" data-end="1379">e.g.</strong> <em data-start="1380" data-end="1566">It\'s the volunteers who are the real unsung heroes of this event. They\'re the ones who put in countless hours without pay to ensure that everything runs smoothly from beginning to end.</em></p>\\r\\n<p data-start="1570" data-end="1789"><strong data-start="1570" data-end="1589">6. Word Records</strong>: A well-known Christian music label in the U.S. Word Records has been a giant in the field of Christian music for decades. It was founded in 1951 in Waco, Texas, by Jarrell F. McCracken.</p>',
                'transcript' => '<p><strong data-start="92" data-end="105">1. Eddie:</strong> When you\'re all done here, Susan will be here&hellip; to square away. Cash, right?<br data-start="181" data-end="184"><strong data-start="184" data-end="197">2. David:</strong> Yes.<br data-start="202" data-end="205"><strong data-start="205" data-end="218" data-is-only-node="">3. Eddie:</strong> Huh? No, I was talking to the cleaning guy. No, I&rsquo;m just saying, don&rsquo;t take it personally&hellip;<br data-start="309" data-end="312"><strong data-start="312" data-end="327">4. Rebecca:</strong> You know Mr. DeGarmo started a record label.<br data-start="372" data-end="375"><strong data-start="375" data-end="388">5. Helen:</strong> Really?<br data-start="396" data-end="399"><strong data-start="399" data-end="414">6. Rebecca:</strong> Yeah.<br data-start="420" data-end="423"><strong data-start="423" data-end="436">7. Helen:</strong> Did you know that David?<br data-start="461" data-end="464"><strong data-start="464" data-end="477">8. David:</strong> I did.<br data-start="484" data-end="487"><strong data-start="487" data-end="502">9. Rebecca:</strong> I thought we could ask him for an audition. Since he is a friend.<br data-start="568" data-end="571"><strong data-start="571" data-end="585">10. Helen:</strong> Yeah! I don&rsquo;t see why not.<br data-start="612" data-end="615"><strong data-start="615" data-end="629">11. David:</strong> I don&rsquo;t think it&rsquo;s a good idea.<br data-start="661" data-end="664"><strong data-start="664" data-end="678">12. Helen:</strong> Why isn&rsquo;t it a good idea?<br data-start="704" data-end="707"><strong data-start="707" data-end="721">13. David:</strong> Look, we&rsquo;ve got the Word Records audition. I say we focus on that, I feel good about it.<br data-start="810" data-end="813"><strong data-start="813" data-end="829">14. Rebecca:</strong> Yeah, but we felt good about other ones too. So if it doesn&rsquo;t work out I just&hellip;<br data-start="908" data-end="911"><strong data-start="911" data-end="925">15. David:</strong> I don&rsquo;t think it&rsquo;s the right fit.<br data-start="959" data-end="962"><strong data-start="962" data-end="976">16. Helen:</strong> Well, beggars can&rsquo;t be choosers, David.<br data-start="1016" data-end="1019"><strong data-start="1019" data-end="1033">17. David:</strong> We are not all beggars, Helen.<br data-start="1064" data-end="1067"><strong data-start="1067" data-end="1081">18. Helen:</strong> What is that supposed to mean?<br data-start="1112" data-end="1115"><strong data-start="1115" data-end="1129">19. David:</strong> It means I know this business. And I&rsquo;m not begging Eddie or anyone.<br data-start="1197" data-end="1200"><strong data-start="1200" data-end="1216">20. Rebecca:</strong> I&rsquo;m not asking you to beg, dad. I just thought we could talk to him since he is a friend.<br data-start="1306" data-end="1309"><strong data-start="1309" data-end="1323">21. David:</strong> He is not your friend.<br data-start="1346" data-end="1349"><strong data-start="1349" data-end="1365">22. Rebecca:</strong> Why not just give it a go? I don&rsquo;t understand&hellip;<br data-start="1412" data-end="1415"><strong data-start="1415" data-end="1429">23. David:</strong> Because you are his maid! &hellip; You are just his maid, Bec. That&rsquo;s all you are to him.. Bec, look, I only meant&hellip;<br data-start="1538" data-end="1541"><strong data-start="1541" data-end="1555">24. Helen:</strong> You bite her head off for trying, you are being too hard on her.<br data-start="1620" data-end="1623"><strong data-start="1623" data-end="1637">25. David:</strong> Compared to what, Helen? Compared to you? I wanted to protect her from this, but you pushed for it. So now someone&rsquo;s gotta teach her and all of them to face facts.<br data-start="1801" data-end="1804"><strong data-start="1804" data-end="1818">26. Helen:</strong> You don&rsquo;t need to tell me that I&rsquo;m a bad mom cause I already feel like I&rsquo;m failing them every day.<br data-start="1917" data-end="1920"><strong data-start="1920" data-end="1934">27. David:</strong> None thinks you are a bad mom. Everyone knows who the real failure is.<br data-start="2005" data-end="2008"><strong data-start="2008" data-end="2022">28. Helen:</strong> Oh such pride! So much pride!<br data-start="2052" data-end="2055"><strong data-start="2055" data-end="2069">29. David:</strong> Yeah right. So much pride that I just scrubbed Eddie DeGarmo&rsquo;s toilet. But hey, that&rsquo;s not degrading enough, now I have to go down on bend the knee and ask him for a record deal?<br data-start="2248" data-end="2251"><strong data-start="2251" data-end="2265">30. Helen:</strong> She just wants an audition.<br data-start="2293" data-end="2296"><strong data-start="2296" data-end="2310">31. David:</strong> That&rsquo;s not the point.<br data-start="2332" data-end="2335"><strong data-start="2335" data-end="2349">32. Helen:</strong> You are not protecting her! You are not protecting any of them. You are protecting your pride. And it\'s holding them back! Just because you had your dreams dashed doesn&rsquo;t mean I&rsquo;m gonna stand here and let you crush theirs, David&hellip;<br data-start="2579" data-end="2582"><strong data-start="2582" data-end="2596">33. David:</strong> She needs to hear what I wish to God someone had told me: people in this industry, they are not your friends. They do not care about you or what happens to you&hellip;<br data-start="2757" data-end="2760"><strong data-start="2760" data-end="2774">34. Helen:</strong> David &hellip;<br data-start="2782" data-end="2785"><strong data-start="2785" data-end="2799">35. David:</strong> They only care about what you can do for them and it\'s not enough! It will never be enough. You will never be enough!<br data-start="2917" data-end="2920"><strong data-start="2920" data-end="2934">36. Helen:</strong> David! Stop it! Get out. You need to leave. Leave!</p>',
                'glossary' => '<p data-start="57" data-end="199"><strong>1.</strong><strong data-start="57" data-end="76"> achieve </strong>(verb): To successfully do something after trying hard. <strong data-start="125" data-end="133">e.g.</strong> <em data-start="134" data-end="197" data-is-only-node="">She studied hard and achieved her dream of becoming a doctor.</em></p>\\r\\n<p data-start="57" data-end="199"><strong>2. </strong><strong data-start="204" data-end="222">advice </strong>(noun): Helpful words someone gives you when you have to make a decision. <strong data-start="289" data-end="297">e.g.</strong> <em data-start="298" data-end="357" data-is-only-node="">The teacher&rsquo;s advice helped me prepare well for the exam.</em></p>\\r\\n<p data-start="364" data-end="531"><strong data-start="364" data-end="382">3. afford </strong>(verb): To have enough money (or time) to buy or do something. <strong data-start="438" data-end="446">e.g.</strong> <em data-start="447" data-end="529" data-is-only-node="">I love this wedding dress, but it is too expensive! I can&rsquo;t afford it right now.</em></p>\\r\\n<p data-start="536" data-end="657"><strong data-start="536" data-end="554">4. appear </strong>(verb): Seems to be or looks like something. <strong data-start="592" data-end="600">e.g.</strong> <em data-start="601" data-end="655" data-is-only-node="">She appears tired today&mdash;maybe she didn&rsquo;t sleep well.</em></p>\\r\\n<p data-start="536" data-end="657"><strong data-start="662" data-end="685">5. arrangement </strong>(noun): Plans you make to get ready for something. <strong data-start="729" data-end="737">e.g.</strong> <em data-start="738" data-end="790" data-is-only-node="">We made all the arrangements for our holiday trip.</em></p>\\r\\n<p data-start="797" data-end="927"><strong data-start="797" data-end="821">6. ashamed </strong>(adjective): Feeling bad or guilty about something you did. <strong data-start="869" data-end="877">e.g.</strong> <em data-start="878" data-end="925" data-is-only-node="">He felt ashamed for lying to his best friend.</em></p>\\r\\n<p data-start="178" data-end="317"><strong data-start="178" data-end="194">7. available</strong> (adjective): Ready to be used, taken, or seen. <strong data-start="242" data-end="250">e.g.</strong> <em data-start="251" data-end="315">The doctor is available this afternoon if you want to see her.</em></p>\\r\\n<p data-start="319" data-end="502"><strong data-start="319" data-end="331">8. avoid</strong> (verb): to prevent something from happening or to not allow yourself to do something. <strong data-start="418" data-end="426">e.g.</strong> <em data-start="427" data-end="500">I try to avoid going shopping on Saturdays because it gets too crowded.</em></p>\\r\\n<p data-start="504" data-end="608"><strong data-start="504" data-end="518">9. beggars</strong> (noun). <strong data-start="526" data-end="534">e.g.</strong> <em data-start="535" data-end="606">In my city, jobs are hard to find, and many end up as street beggars.</em></p>\\r\\n<p data-start="504" data-end="608"><strong>10.&nbsp; beggar can\'t be choosers: </strong>(idiom) people who are in desperate need (the &ldquo;beggars&rdquo;) cannot be selective or picky (&ldquo;choosers&rdquo;) about what they receive. <strong>e.g. </strong><em>I didn&rsquo;t really like the soup, but I ate it because, as they say, beggars can&rsquo;t be choosers.</em></p>\\r\\n<p data-start="610" data-end="742"><strong data-start="610" data-end="635">11. be hard on someone</strong> (phrase). <strong data-start="645" data-end="653">e.g.</strong> <em data-start="654" data-end="740">A good manager knows exactly when to be hard on players and when to go easy on them.</em></p>\\r\\n<p data-start="744" data-end="900"><strong data-start="744" data-end="764">12. bend the knee</strong> (idiom). <strong data-start="773" data-end="781">e.g.</strong> <em data-start="782" data-end="898">He would rather struggle in silence than bend the knee for help, too proud to admit he needed anyone\'s assistance.</em></p>\\r\\n<p data-start="902" data-end="1101"><strong data-start="902" data-end="915">13. biased</strong> (adjective): Not fair&mdash;picking favorites because you prefer one thing or person too much. <strong data-start="1005" data-end="1013">e.g.</strong> <em data-start="1014" data-end="1099">If you like one team too much, you might be biased when giving opinions about them.</em></p>\\r\\n<p data-start="1103" data-end="1247"><strong data-start="1103" data-end="1115">14. crush</strong> (noun): A strong, usually short-term feeling of love for someone. <strong data-start="1182" data-end="1190">e.g.</strong> <em data-start="1191" data-end="1245">He has a crush on her but is too shy to talk to her.</em></p>\\r\\n<p data-start="1249" data-end="1485"><strong data-start="1249" data-end="1260">15. dash</strong> (verb): To break or destroy something suddenly (used figuratively); ruined or crushed (especially hopes or plans). <strong data-start="1376" data-end="1384">e.g.</strong> <em data-start="1385" data-end="1483">She dreamed of becoming a doctor, but when she failed the entrance exam, her dreams were dashed.</em></p>\\r\\n<p data-start="1487" data-end="1663"><strong data-start="1487" data-end="1503">16. deal with</strong> (phrasal verb): To take action to manage, solve, or handle a situation, person, or problem. <strong data-start="1596" data-end="1604">e.g.</strong> <em data-start="1605" data-end="1661">The manager had to deal with several complaints today.</em></p>\\r\\n<p data-start="1665" data-end="1811"><strong data-start="1665" data-end="1687">17. disappointment</strong> (noun): Times when things don\'t go the way you hoped. <strong data-start="1742" data-end="1750">e.g.</strong> <em data-start="1751" data-end="1809">Life is full of disappointments, but we learn from them.</em></p>\\r\\n<p data-start="1813" data-end="1992"><strong data-start="1813" data-end="1832">18. discouraged</strong> (adjective): Feeling like you can&rsquo;t do something anymore because it&rsquo;s too hard. <strong data-start="1913" data-end="1921">e.g.</strong> <em data-start="1922" data-end="1990">He felt discouraged after failing the test, but he didn&rsquo;t give up.</em></p>\\r\\n<p data-start="1994" data-end="2166"><strong data-start="1994" data-end="2015">19. embarrassment</strong> (noun): A feeling of being uncomfortable or shy because of something awkward. <strong data-start="2094" data-end="2102">e.g.</strong> <em data-start="2103" data-end="2164">She fell in front of the class and felt deep embarrassment.</em></p>\\r\\n<p data-start="2168" data-end="2283"><strong data-start="2168" data-end="2182">20. enough</strong> (pronoun) <strong data-start="2193" data-end="2201">e.g.</strong> <em data-start="2202" data-end="2281">There are 25 textbooks per class. That should be enough for all the students.</em></p>\\r\\n<p data-start="2285" data-end="2418"><strong data-start="2285" data-end="2303">21. face facts</strong> (idiom) <strong data-start="2312" data-end="2320">e.g.</strong> <em data-start="2321" data-end="2416">You have to face facts; your business isn\'t making enough money, and changes need to be made.</em></p>\\r\\n<p data-start="2420" data-end="2628"><strong data-start="2420" data-end="2435">22. failure</strong> (noun): The fact of not achieving what you wanted to do or were expected to do. <strong data-start="2516" data-end="2524">e.g.</strong> <em data-start="2525" data-end="2626">Unfortunately, the project was a failure&mdash;we didn&rsquo;t achieve any of the goals, not even the deadline.</em></p>\\r\\n<p data-start="2630" data-end="2773"><strong data-start="2630" data-end="2650">23. give it a go</strong> (idiom) <strong data-start="2659" data-end="2667">e.g.</strong> <em data-start="2668" data-end="2771">She\'s been thinking about learning to sing for many years, and she\'s finally decided to give it a go.</em></p>\\r\\n<p data-start="2775" data-end="2906"><strong data-start="2775" data-end="2790">24. give up</strong> (phrasal verb): To stop trying because it&rsquo;s hard. <strong data-start="2841" data-end="2849">e.g.</strong> <em data-start="2850" data-end="2904">Don&rsquo;t give up&mdash;keep practicing and you&rsquo;ll get better!</em></p>\\r\\n<p data-start="2908" data-end="3111"><strong data-start="2908" data-end="2927">25. harsh truth</strong> (noun phrase): A fact or reality that is difficult, painful, or unpleasant to accept, even though it is true. <strong data-start="3038" data-end="3046">e.g.</strong> <em data-start="3047" data-end="3109">The harsh truth is that some friendships don\'t last forever.</em></p>\\r\\n<p data-start="3113" data-end="3217"><strong data-start="3113" data-end="3138">26. hold someone back</strong> (phrasal verb) <strong data-start="3154" data-end="3162">e.g.</strong> <em data-start="3163" data-end="3215">She felt that having children would hold her back.</em></p>\\r\\n<p data-start="3219" data-end="3460"><strong data-start="3219" data-end="3240">27. hold together</strong> (phrasal verb): To maintain unity, stability, or coherence, preventing something from breaking apart or becoming disorganized. <strong data-start="3368" data-end="3376">e.g.</strong> <em data-start="3377" data-end="3458">During difficult times, it\'s important for friends to hold each other together.</em></p>\\r\\n<p data-start="3462" data-end="3612"><strong data-start="3462" data-end="3478">28. jealousy</strong> (noun): A feeling of wanting what someone else has. <strong data-start="3531" data-end="3539">e.g.</strong> <em data-start="3540" data-end="3610">She felt jealousy when her best friend spent time with someone else.</em></p>\\r\\n<p data-start="3614" data-end="3807"><strong data-start="3614" data-end="3632">29. kneel down</strong> (phrasal verb): To put one or both knees on the ground, often to show respect, prayer, or to do something close to the floor. <strong data-start="3759" data-end="3767">e.g.</strong> <em data-start="3768" data-end="3805">He knelt down to pray at the altar.</em></p>\\r\\n<p data-start="3809" data-end="3963"><strong data-start="3809" data-end="3821">30. maid</strong> (noun): A person (usually a woman) who cleans or works in someone&rsquo;s home. <strong data-start="3896" data-end="3904">e.g.</strong> <em data-start="3905" data-end="3961">The maid comes every week to help clean the apartment.</em></p>\\r\\n<p data-start="3965" data-end="4235"><strong data-start="3965" data-end="3985">31. move forward</strong> (expression): Refers to progressing, continuing, or focusing on the future after something has happened&mdash;especially after a problem, decision, or change. <strong data-start="4139" data-end="4147">e.g.</strong> <em data-start="4148" data-end="4233">She decided to stop thinking about the past and start moving forward with her life.</em></p>\\r\\n<p data-start="4237" data-end="4444"><strong data-start="4237" data-end="4253">32. prideful</strong> (adjective): Feeling or showing too much pride in yourself &mdash; sometimes in a way that seems arrogant or overly confident. <strong data-start="4375" data-end="4383">e.g.</strong> <em data-start="4384" data-end="4442">She gave a prideful smile after winning the competition.</em></p>\\r\\n<p data-start="4446" data-end="4710"><strong data-start="4446" data-end="4465">33. push for it</strong> (phrase): To try very hard or to take a risk to achieve something, sometimes going beyond what is comfortable or safe. <strong data-start="4585" data-end="4593">e.g.</strong> <em data-start="4594" data-end="4708">She pushed for it during the project presentation, insisting her ideas were the best even when others disagreed.</em></p>\\r\\n<p data-start="4712" data-end="4879"><strong data-start="4712" data-end="4724">34. quit</strong> (verb): To stop doing something, usually something regular like a job or a habit. <strong data-start="4807" data-end="4815">e.g.</strong> <em data-start="4816" data-end="4877">He quit the soccer team because he didn&rsquo;t enjoy it anymore.</em></p>\\r\\n<p data-start="4881" data-end="5028"><strong data-start="4881" data-end="4900">35. record deal</strong> (noun): A contract to make and sell music with a company. <strong data-start="4959" data-end="4967">e.g.</strong> <em data-start="4968" data-end="5026">The singer got a record deal after posting songs online.</em></p>\\r\\n<p data-start="5030" data-end="5238"><strong data-start="5030" data-end="5050">36. record label</strong> (noun): A company that produces, promotes, and sells music recordings by artists and bands. <strong data-start="5143" data-end="5151">e.g.</strong> <em data-start="5152" data-end="5236">The singer signed a contract with a major record label to release her first album.</em></p>\\r\\n<p data-start="5240" data-end="5368"><strong data-start="5240" data-end="5254">37. refuse</strong> (verb): Someone who says no or does not accept or agree to something. <strong data-start="5325" data-end="5333">e.g.</strong> <em data-start="5334" data-end="5366">She refuses to eat vegetables.</em></p>\\r\\n<p data-start="5370" data-end="5553"><strong data-start="5370" data-end="5386">38. rejected</strong> (adjective): Not given approval or acceptance. <strong data-start="5434" data-end="5442">e.g.</strong> <em data-start="5443" data-end="5551">After numerous job interviews, he felt increasingly rejected and discouraged by the lack of opportunities.</em></p>\\r\\n<p data-start="5555" data-end="5668"><strong data-start="5555" data-end="5568">39. scrub</strong> (verb): To clean something by rubbing hard. <strong data-start="5613" data-end="5621">e.g.</strong> <em data-start="5622" data-end="5666">She scrubbed the floor until it was shiny.</em></p>\\r\\n<p data-start="5670" data-end="5780"><strong data-start="5670" data-end="5685">40. several</strong> (determiner): More than two but not many; a few. <strong data-start="5735" data-end="5743">e.g.</strong> <em data-start="5744" data-end="5778">I have several books on my desk.</em></p>\\r\\n<p data-start="5782" data-end="5927"><strong data-start="5782" data-end="5798">41. suitable</strong> (adjective): Right or appropriate for a particular purpose or situation. <strong data-start="5872" data-end="5880">e.g.</strong> <em data-start="5881" data-end="5925">This dress is suitable for a formal party.</em></p>\\r\\n<p data-start="5929" data-end="6062"><strong data-start="5929" data-end="5950">42. the right fit</strong> (phrase) <strong data-start="5960" data-end="5968">e.g.</strong> <em data-start="5969" data-end="6060">We\'re looking for a candidate who is the right fit for our team culture and expectations.</em></p>\\r\\n<p data-start="6064" data-end="6290"><strong data-start="6064" data-end="6080">43. unfairly</strong> (adverb): In a way that is not just, reasonable, or equal; treating someone or something without fairness. <strong data-start="6188" data-end="6196">e.g.</strong> <em data-start="6197" data-end="6288">They were treated unfairly during the competition when the judges favored the other team.</em></p>',
