            [
                'id' => 4005,
                'title' => 'U6: Play hard, be tough',
                'author' => 'Whiplash',
                'description' => '<p>In this unit, you will complete several comprehension exercises about <strong id="docs-internal-guid-2c368aff-7fff-1ab6-411b-9a649006df94">&nbsp;</strong>a conversation between Mr. Fletcher, an orchestra conductor, and Andrew, an aspiring drummer. You will also define your goals, identify persistent comprehension problems and reflect on strategies to compensate for them.</p>',
                'listening_tips' => null,
                'cultural_notes' => '<p>1. <strong>The Bird</strong> (noun): Charlie Parker&rsquo;s nickname, originally &ldquo;Yardbird,&rdquo; inspired by his love for chicken and a car accident involving one. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>People say that when Bird played, it felt like the music could fly &mdash; just like his nickname.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>2. <strong>Charlie Parker</strong> (noun): American jazz saxophonist, bandleader, and composer known for revolutionizing jazz. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>Today, Charlie Parker is remembered as one of the most important and innovative figures in jazz history.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>3. <strong>Cutting session</strong> (noun): A friendly but competitive jam session where musicians try to outplay each other. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>Some of the best jazz recordings were born during spontaneous cutting sessions between rival players.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>4. <strong>Jazz</strong> (noun): A genre of music developed by African-American musicians, often featuring improvisation and syncopated rhythms. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>They booked a jazz band for their wedding.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>5. <strong>JVC Fest</strong> (noun): An annual jazz festival originally held in Newport, Rhode Island, and later in New York City. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>The JVC Festival helped keep jazz alive by celebrating both traditional styles and modern innovations.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>6. <strong>Louis Armstrong</strong> (noun): American trumpeter and vocalist nicknamed "Satchmo," "Satch," and "Pops"; a major figure in jazz history. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>Louis Armstrong&rsquo;s version of &ldquo;What a Wonderful World&rdquo; remains one of the most beloved songs of all time.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>7. <strong>Peaches and cream</strong> (expression): Describes something going smoothly or someone who is sweet and easygoing. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>Don&rsquo;t be fooled by his peaches-and-cream attitude; he can be quite tough when needed.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>8. <strong>Pro band</strong> (noun): A professional jazz group that performs regularly and earns income from their music. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>You can tell they&rsquo;re a pro band &mdash; their timing, sound, and stage presence are all perfect.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>9. <strong>Shaffer</strong> (noun): A fictional elite music school featured in the film Whiplash. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>He must be a great drummer because he is studying at Shaffer.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>10. <strong>Solo</strong> (noun): A musical performance by a single musician, either alone or with accompaniment. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>The guitarist played an amazing solo in the middle of the song that impressed everyone.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>11. <strong>Third trumpet</strong> (noun): The third part or position in a trumpet section of a band or ensemble. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>He started in the school band as third trumpet but worked his way up to lead trumpet.</em></p>',
                'transcript' => '<p dir="ltr">1. <strong>Terence: </strong>I don\'t know if you heard.I\'m not at Shaffer anymore</p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">2. <strong>Andrew:</strong> Yeah, I did hear that. Did you quit?</p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">3. <strong>Terence:</strong> Not exactly. Some parents got a kid from Sean Casey\'s year, I think, to say some things about me. Although why would anybody&nbsp; have anything other than peaches and cream to say about me. It&rsquo;s a mystery.That\'s a good laugh, right?&nbsp;</p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">4. <strong>Andrew:</strong> I\'m sorry. No, I\'m sorry.</p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">5. <strong>Terence:</strong> I get it. I know I made enemies.I\'m conducting an LOL.They brought back the JVC fest this year. They got me opening in a couple of weeks with a pro band.</p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">6. <strong>Andrew:</strong> That\'s great.&nbsp;</p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">7. <strong>Terence:</strong> It&rsquo;s alright. Truth is, I don\'t think people understood what it was I was doing at Schaffer, I wasn\'t there to conduct. Any fucking moron can wave his arms and keep people in tempo. I was there to push people beyond what\'s expected of them. I believe that is an absolute necessity. Otherwise, we\'re depriving the world of the next Louis Armstrong, the next Charlie Parker. I told you that story about how Charlie Parker became Charlie Parker, right?</p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">8. <strong>Andrew:</strong> Jo Jones threw a cymbal at his head.&nbsp;</p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">9. <strong>Terence:</strong> Exactly. Parker\'s a young kid, pretty good on the sax, gets up to play at a cutting session and he fucks it up. And Jones nearly decapitates him for it. And he\'s laughed off stage.Cries himself to sleep that night, but the next morning, what does he do? He practices and he practices and he practices with one goal in mind, never to be laughed at again. And a year later, he goes back to the Reno and he steps up on that stage and he plays the best motherfucking solo the world has ever heard. So imagine if Jones had just said &ldquo;Well, that\'s OK, Charlie I.That was alright, good job&rdquo; But Charlie thinks to himself &ldquo;Well, shit, I did do a pretty good job&rdquo; End of story. No bird.That to me is an absolute tragedy. But that\'s just what the world wants now. No wonder why jazz is dying. I tell you man, and every Starbucks jazz album just proves my point really. There are no two words in the English language more harmful than &ldquo;good job&rdquo;&nbsp;</p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">10. <strong>Andrew:</strong> But is there a line? You know, maybe you go too far and you discourage the next Charlie Parker from ever becoming Charlie Parker.</p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">11. <strong>Terence:</strong> No, man, no, because the next Charlie Parker would never be discouraged.&nbsp;</p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">12. <strong>Andrew: </strong>Yeah.</p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">13. <strong>Terence:</strong> The truth is Andrew I never really had a Charlie Parker, but I tried.I actually fucking tried and that\'s more than most people ever do, and I would never apologize for how I tried.&nbsp;</p>',
                'glossary' => '<p>1. <strong>Aim to </strong>(verb): to try or plan to do something, or to have a goal or intention. <strong>e.g. </strong><em>She aims to become a professional musician one day.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>2. <strong>Awkward</strong> (adjective): difficult to use, do, or deal with <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>I felt awkward when I didn&rsquo;t know what to say at the party.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>3. <strong>Baton</strong> (noun): a stick used by a conductor <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>The conductor waved his baton for the orchestra to play.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>4. <strong>A drive in someone</strong> (noun): a strong motivation, determination, or inner energy that pushes someone to achieve something or work hard <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>There was a drive in him that kept him practicing every day, no matter how tired he was.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>5. <strong>Come across </strong>(verb): to find or meet something or someone by chance, or to seem or appear in a certain way. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>I came across an old photo album while cleaning the attic.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>6. <strong>Curse oneself</strong> (verb): to feel angry or frustrated with yourself, usually because you made a mistake or missed an opportunity <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>I cursed myself after realizing I had sent the email to the wrong person.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>7. <strong>Embarrassment </strong>(noun): a feeling of self-consciousness, shame, or awkwardness <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>I spilled juice on my shirt, and my face turned red with embarrassment.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>8. <strong>Encourage</strong> (verb): the act of giving someone support, confidence, or hope to help them keep going or try something new <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>Teachers should encourage students to ask questions and think critically.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>9. <strong>Be fired</strong> (verb): dismiss (an employee) from a job. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>He was fired because he was always late for work.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>10. <strong>Greatness</strong> (noun): it implies a level of achievement or quality that sets something or someone apart as being notably superior or impressive&nbsp;<strong>e.g.</strong> <em>Her greatness as a writer is unquestioned, none writes like her.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>11. <strong>Harmful</strong> (adjective): <strong>e.g. </strong><em>That comment was really harmful to her self-esteem.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>12. <strong>Laugh at </strong>(verb): <strong>e.g. </strong><em>She laughed at the movie because it was so ridiculous.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>13. <strong>Laugh off</strong> (verb): <strong>e.g. </strong><em>She laughed off the criticism and kept working hard.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>14. <strong>Make accusations against</strong> (verb): to claim that someone has done something wrong <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>She made some accusations against Phil for stealing her money.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>15. <strong>Mistake</strong> (noun): be wrong about something <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>I made a mistake on my test, but the teacher helped me correct it.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>16. <strong>Mock someone</strong> (verb): to laugh at or copy someone in a mean or unkind way, usually to make fun of them. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>The other kids mocked him for wearing old shoes, which made him feel embarrassed.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>17. <strong>Moron </strong>(noun): <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>I felt like a moron when I realized I&rsquo;d been using the wrong password all day.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>18. <strong>Praise</strong> (verb): to say good things about someone or something because you admire or approve of them. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>The teacher praised the student for her hard work on the project.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>19. <strong>Resilience</strong> (noun): the ability to recover quickly after something bad happens, like a failure or difficulty. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>After losing the game, the team\'s resilience helped them stay positive and train harder.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>20. <strong>Skinny</strong> (adjective): very thin, sometimes too thin. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>He was so skinny that his clothes looked too big for him.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>21. <strong>Squeak in</strong> (verb): to succeed in doing something just in time or with difficulty. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>She squeaked in before the deadline by submitting her paper one minute early.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>22. <strong>Stay in the background</strong> (idiom): to not get attention or take control; to let others lead or be noticed. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>During the group project, he stayed in the background and let his classmates present the ideas.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>23. <strong>Tough</strong> (adjective): rough, hard and violent <strong>e.g. </strong><em>The exam was really tough, but I think I passed.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>24. <strong>Struggling</strong> (adjective): having difficulty doing something, especially because it\'s hard or you&rsquo;re not succeeding. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>Many students are struggling to keep up with the fast pace of the course.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>25. <strong>Upset someone</strong> (verb): to make someone feel sad, worried, or angry. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>His comment really upset her, and she left the room without speaking.</em></p>',
                'translation' => null,
                'dictionary' => '<p><a href="https://dictionary.cambridge.org/es/diccionario/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cambridge dictionary</a></p>',
                'listening_tips_enabled' => true,
