            [
                'id' => 4004,
                'title' => 'U5: Stay in tune',
                'author' => 'Music & lyrics',
                'description' => '<p>In this unit, you will <strong>complete </strong>several comprehension exercises about <strong>a washed-up pop star, Alex Fletcher, who gets an unexpected opportunity to write a song for a young superstar, Cora.</strong> You will also <strong>define </strong>your goals, <strong>identify </strong>persistent comprehension problems and <strong>reflect </strong>on strategies to compensate for them.</p>',
                'listening_tips' => null,
                'cultural_notes' => '<p>1.<strong> Indiana State Fair&nbsp;</strong>(noun)<strong>:</strong> The Indiana State Fair is an annual state fair that spans 15 days in August [1] in Indianapolis, Indiana, US. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>We visited the Indiana State Fair last summer&mdash;it had amazing food stalls, fun rides, and a live concert every night.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>2.<strong> Texas and Arkansas&nbsp;</strong>(nouns)<strong>:</strong> refers to two states of the United States. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>I visited Texas and Arkansas last year. Both had beautiful landscapes and fun things to do.&nbsp;<br></em></p>\\r\\n<p>3.<strong> PoP&nbsp;</strong>(noun)<strong>:&nbsp;</strong>refers to a big hit song written by the artist in the movie, Alex Fletcher. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>&ldquo;PoP!&rdquo; was such a catchy song in the movie.&nbsp;</em></p>\\r\\n<p>4.<strong> Tears for Fear&nbsp;</strong>(noun)<strong>:</strong> are an English pop rock band formed in 1981.<strong> e.g.</strong><em> My dad still listens to Tears for Fears&mdash;his favorite song is &ldquo;Everybody Wants to Rule the World.&rdquo;</em></p>\\r\\n<p>5.<strong> Spice Girls&nbsp;</strong>(noun)<strong>:</strong> are an English girl group formed in 1994. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>When I was younger, I loved the Spice Girls and always sang &ldquo;Wannabe&rdquo; with my friends.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>6.<strong> Bar mitzvahs&nbsp;</strong>(noun)<strong>:</strong> is a Jewish coming-of-age ceremony for boys, marking their entrance into religious adulthood when they turn 13. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>Last weekend, we attended Jacob&rsquo;s bar mitzvah. It was a beautiful ceremony followed by a big party.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>7. <strong>Knott&rsquo;s Berry and Great Adventure&nbsp;</strong>(noun)<strong>:</strong> is a popular theme park. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>We spent the whole day at Knott&rsquo;s Berry Farm riding roller coasters and eating boysenberry pie.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>8.<strong> Disneyland&nbsp;</strong>(noun)<strong>:</strong> is the most famous theme park in the USA and worldwide. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>Going to Disneyland was a dream come true! I even got to take a photo with Mickey Mouse.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>9.<strong> Guy&nbsp;</strong>(noun)<strong>:</strong> is a term used to refer to a man or a boy in informal English. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>That guy over there helped me find my phone when I dropped it&mdash;so kind of him!</em></p>\\r\\n<p>10.<strong> Avril&nbsp;</strong>(noun)<strong>:</strong> Avril Lavigne is a Canadian singer and songwriter who punk-influenced pop music in the early 2000s. <strong>e.g. </strong><em>Avril Lavigne&rsquo;s &ldquo;Complicated&rdquo; was always on the radio when I was a teenager.</em></p>',
                'transcript' => '<p>1. <strong>Ray:</strong> Hey, how&rsquo;s it going I&rsquo;m Ray, Cora&rsquo;s manager</p>\\r\\n<p>2. <strong>Chris Riley:</strong> Hey, Chris Riley</p>\\r\\n<p>3. <strong>Ray: </strong>Nice to meet you</p>\\r\\n<p>4. <strong>Chris Riley:</strong> You know Alex Fletcher</p>\\r\\n<p>5. <strong>Ray:</strong> Great to meet you</p>\\r\\n<p>6. <strong>Alex Fletcher:</strong> Hi, Ray, um&hellip;?</p>\\r\\n<p>7. <strong>Ray:</strong> Uh, just Ray. Cora&rsquo;s this way. Come on. Follow me, please. C, this is Alex Fletcher and his manager, Chris Riley.</p>\\r\\n<p>8. <strong>Chris Riley:</strong> We loved the video it was unbelievable&nbsp;</p>\\r\\n<p>9. <strong>Alex Fletcher:</strong> Yeah!</p>\\r\\n<p>10.<strong> Chris Riley:</strong> I wish I brought my daughter; she worships you. I&rsquo;m divorced, but&nbsp;that&rsquo;s another story.</p>\\r\\n<p>11. <strong>Cora:</strong> Mr. Fletcher, it&rsquo;s a pleasure. Your song &ldquo;Dance with me tonight&rdquo; got me through my parents&rsquo; divorce when I was 7.</p>\\r\\n<p>12. <strong>Alex Fletcher:</strong> Really? Wow! Yeah, because I recorded that when I was 9, so&hellip; Yeah.</p>\\r\\n<p>13. <strong>Cora:</strong> I want my fans to know the same spiritual uplift that your music gave me.</p>\\r\\n<p>14. <strong>Alex Fletcher:</strong> That would be lovely. I actually have a few tunes, it would be nice, to update.</p>\\r\\n<p>15. <strong>Cora:</strong> Oh, I don&rsquo;t live in the past, Mr. Fletcher&hellip; it was so long ago. I want you to write a new song.</p>\\r\\n<p>16. <strong>Alex Fletcher:</strong> Ah, okay.</p>\\r\\n<p>17. <strong>Cora:</strong> You see, um&hellip; I recently broke up with my boyfriend. We had been together for almost two months.</p>\\r\\n<p>18. <strong>Alex Fletcher: </strong>Oh, I&rsquo;m sorry.</p>\\r\\n<p>19. <strong>Cora:</strong> It was a terrible experience but then I read a book by Guru Mathashavi called A Way Back Into Love and that will be the title of our new song. And in two weeks when I open my tour at Madison Square Garden, we&rsquo;ll perform it together.</p>\\r\\n<p>20. <strong>Alex Fletcher:</strong> Okay here&rsquo;s the snag&hellip;</p>\\r\\n<p>21. <strong>Ray:</strong> We also wanna put the song on her new CD which is pretty much finished so we need it by Friday.</p>\\r\\n<p>22. <strong>Alex Fletcher:</strong> You talking about this Friday?</p>\\r\\n<p>23. <strong>Ray:</strong> But don&rsquo;t feel any pressure. I mean we&rsquo;ve got seven other retro artists working on &ldquo;Way Back Into Love&rdquo; so if you blow it, we&rsquo;re covered.</p>\\r\\n<p>24. <strong>Cora:</strong> Mr. Fletcher, don&rsquo;t look at this as a competition. If it&rsquo;s meant to be, it will be. It&rsquo;s destiny.</p>\\r\\n<p>25. <strong>Alex Fletcher:</strong> Yes.</p>\\r\\n<p>26. <strong>Cora:</strong> Or not.</p>\\r\\n<p>27. <strong>Alex Fletcher:</strong> I can&rsquo;t possibly write a song by Friday. What could she be thinking of?</p>\\r\\n<p>28. <strong>Chris Riley:</strong> All right, look, look, can I be honest with you?</p>\\r\\n<p>29. <strong>Alex Fletcher:</strong> Of course not, you&rsquo;re my manager. I would have to fire you immediately.</p>\\r\\n<p>30. <strong>Chris Riley: </strong>We need this.</p>\\r\\n<p>31. <strong>Alex Fletcher:</strong> Let&rsquo;s not be desperate. We have the state fairs tour coming up and Knott&rsquo;s Berry Farm.</p>\\r\\n<p>32. <strong>Chris Riley:</strong> They&rsquo;ve cancelled.</p>\\r\\n<p>33. <strong>Alex Fletcher:</strong> Knott&rsquo;s Berry cancelled?&nbsp;</p>\\r\\n<p>34. <strong>Chris Riley: </strong>Look&hellip; we&rsquo;re still on for the Indiana State Fair, okay? But Texas and Arkansas dropped us.</p>\\r\\n<p>35. <strong>Alex Fletcher:</strong> The Apple Picking?</p>\\r\\n<p>36. <strong>Chris Riley:</strong> The Apple Picking Festival is a go, but Great Adventure only wants three nights instead of ten. All right?</p>\\r\\n<p>37. <strong>Alex Fletcher: </strong>Oh my god. I had no idea. Why didn&rsquo;t you tell me these things? Why&hellip;?</p>\\r\\n<p>38. <strong>Chris Riley:</strong> I&hellip; I&rsquo;m telling you now, Alex, it&rsquo;s been 15 years since PoP. There&rsquo;s new old acts coming up all the time. Tears for Fear is going on tour, there&rsquo;s talk of a Spice Girls reunion.</p>\\r\\n<p>39. <strong>Alex Fletcher:</strong> Well that&rsquo;s not my audience.</p>\\r\\n<p>40. <strong>Chris Riley:</strong> Ricky Martin.</p>\\r\\n<p>41. <strong>Alex Fletcher:</strong> I&rsquo;m dead. I&rsquo;m dead. I&rsquo;m dead. I&rsquo;m finished.</p>\\r\\n<p>42. <strong>Chris Riley:</strong> No, you&rsquo;re not&hellip; you&rsquo;re not dead.</p>\\r\\n<p>43. <strong>Alex Fletcher:</strong> I&rsquo;m gonna wind up doing bar mitzvahs.</p>\\r\\n<p>44. <strong>Chris Riley:</strong> No, you&rsquo;re not. Thirteen-year-old kids have no idea who you are.</p>\\r\\n<p>45. <strong>Alex Fletcher:</strong> Well, that&rsquo;s good to know. But what about you? You might actually have to take another client.</p>\\r\\n<p>46. <strong>Chris Riley:</strong> Look, don&rsquo;t worry about me, what we gotta concentrate on is refreshing your image. Then we&rsquo;ll get Knott&rsquo;s Berry and Great Adventure. Who knows? We might even get Disneyland.</p>\\r\\n<p>47. <strong>Alex Fletcher:</strong> Don&rsquo;t tease me. I&rsquo;m very vulnerable at the moment.</p>\\r\\n<p>48. <strong>Chris Riley:</strong>&nbsp;Tell you something Alex, you do a song for Cora, and there is a spot for you in the Magic Kingdom, baby.&nbsp;</p>\\r\\n<p>49. <strong>Alex Fletcher:</strong> Oh, I don&rsquo;t know. Writing a song, I thought I was done with that whole nightmare.&nbsp;</p>\\r\\n<p>50. <strong>Chris Riley: </strong>Just one song. That&rsquo;s all we need. One song.</p>\\r\\n<p>51. <strong>Alex Fletcher:</strong>&nbsp;But it&rsquo;s so&hellip; time-consuming you know? It&rsquo;s&hellip; and I haven&rsquo;t written for ten years&hellip; and I need a lyricist and it&rsquo;s never worked with anyone except Colin.</p>\\r\\n<p>52. <strong>Chris Riley:&nbsp;</strong>Alright, look I know it&rsquo;s not easy to get somebody good this fast, but there&rsquo;s this guy, supposedly he&rsquo;s very hip, very edgy. He just worked with Avril.</p>',
                'glossary' => '<p>1. <strong>audience</strong> (noun): people who watch or listen to a performance. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>The audience clapped loudly after the concert.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>2. <strong>breathe down my neck</strong> (idiom): to watch or pressure someone too closely. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>My boss kept breathing down my neck while I finished the report.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>3.&nbsp;<strong>comeback</strong> (noun): when someone becomes popular or successful again after being gone or failing. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>After years out of the spotlight, the singer made a big comeback with a new hit song.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>4.&nbsp;<strong>blow it </strong>(phrase): <strong>e.g. </strong><em>This is your big chance&mdash;don&rsquo;t blow it!</em></p>\\r\\n<p>5.&nbsp;<strong>break up</strong> (phrasal verb): to end a romantic relationship. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>They decided to break up after five years together.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>6.&nbsp;<strong>drop someone</strong> (phrasal verb): <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>The company dropped the accountant after the scandal.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>7.&nbsp;<strong>eager</strong> (adjective): very excited or ready to do something. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>The recent graduate was eager to start her new job.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>8.&nbsp;<strong>edgy</strong> (adjective): <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>Her new haircut is really edgy and cool.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>9.&nbsp;<strong>genre</strong> (music) (noun): a type or style of music. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>My favorite music genre is jazz.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>10.&nbsp;<strong>get along </strong>(phrasal verb): to have a good relationship with someone. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>Alice gets along well with her coworkers, which makes team projects easier.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>11.&nbsp;<strong>gig</strong> (noun): a job, especially for musicians or performers. <strong>e.g. </strong>T<em>he band has a gig at the local club this weekend.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>12.&nbsp;<strong>guaranteed </strong>(adjective): certain to happen or be true. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>If you study hard, success is not guaranteed, but it&rsquo;s more likely.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>13.&nbsp;<strong>lyricist </strong>(noun): a person who writes songs. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>The lyricist worked closely with the composer to create a powerful ballad.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>14.&nbsp;<strong>long-lasting</strong> (adjective): continuing for a long time. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>They had a long-lasting friendship since childhood.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>15.&nbsp;<strong>mindset</strong> (noun): a person&rsquo;s way of thinking. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>To succeed, you need to have a positive mindset.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>16.&nbsp;<strong>pandering</strong> (noun/verb): saying or doing things just to please others. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>The speech felt like it was just pandering to the audience.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>17.&nbsp;<strong>payment</strong> (noun): money given for work or service. <strong>e.g. </strong><em>The artist received his payment after the concert.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>18.&nbsp;<strong>promote</strong> (verb): to help something become more popular or successful. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>She worked hard to promote her new album online.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>19.&nbsp;<strong>remind</strong> (verb): to help someone remember something.<strong> e.g. </strong><em>Please remind me to call my mom later.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>20.&nbsp;<strong>rehearse</strong> (verb): to practice for a performance. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>The actors will rehearse all week before the play.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>21.&nbsp;<strong>struggle</strong> (noun): a difficult effort to do something. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>Learning English can be a struggle, but it&rsquo;s worth it.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>22.&nbsp;<strong>snag</strong> (noun): <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>We hit a snag while planning the event.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>23.&nbsp;<strong>tease</strong> (verb): to make fun of someone, often in a playful way. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>My brother likes to tease me about my singing.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>24.&nbsp;<strong>to get someone through something</strong> (phrase): <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>Music really got me through my exams.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>25.&nbsp;<strong>tune</strong> (noun): <strong>e.g. </strong><em>My sister can&rsquo;t stop humming that catchy tune from the concert.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>26.&nbsp;<strong>uplift </strong>(noun): <strong>e.g. </strong><em>Her kind words gave me a real uplift.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>27.&nbsp;<strong>washed-up</strong> (adjective): no longer successful, famous, or useful. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>People thought the actor was washed-up, but he surprised everyone with a great new role.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>28.&nbsp;<strong>wind up</strong> (phrasal verb): <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>If you&rsquo;re not careful, you&rsquo;ll wind up late again.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>29.&nbsp;<strong>worship</strong> (verb): <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>Some fans worship pop stars like gods.</em></p>',
                'translation' => null,
                'dictionary' => '<p><a href="https://dictionary.cambridge.org/es/diccionario/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cambridge dictionary</a></p>',
