            [
                'id' => 4001,
                'title' => 'U2: Feel every note',
                'author' => 'Mr. Holland\'s Opus',
                'description' => '<p>In this unit, you will <strong>complete </strong>several comprehension exercises about <strong>Mr. Holland, a music teacher describing the importance of emotions in music.</strong> You will also <strong>define </strong>your goals, <strong>identify </strong>persistent comprehension problems and <strong>reflect </strong>on strategies to compensate for them.</p>',
                'listening_tips' => '<p><strong>Break listening into parts</strong>: First, listen for<strong> the topic</strong>. &nbsp;Then, listen for <strong>names or numbers</strong>. Finally, listen for <strong>feelings or opinions</strong>. Replay short sections &ndash; it&rsquo;s fine to replay once or twice if your goal is to move forward.</p>',
                'cultural_notes' => '<p>1. <strong>Blue ribbon</strong> (noun): an honor or award gained for a great performance at something.&nbsp;<strong>e.g. </strong><em>The band won a blue ribbon at the music festival for their amazing performance.</em>&nbsp;</p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">2. <strong>Three chords</strong> (noun): a reference to basic popular music structure; many rock or pop songs use three-chord progressions. <strong>e.g. </strong><em>That band keeps playing the same three chords, but it is fun!</em></p>',
                'transcript' => '<p dir="ltr"><strong>Miss Lang</strong>: Mr. Holland?</p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr"><strong>Mr. Holland</strong>: You&rsquo;re late.. and you left your clarinet here the other day.</p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr"><strong>Miss Lang</strong>: Yeah, uhm, if you know anyone who wants it&hellip; Uhm, I&rsquo;m giving up the clarinet. I&rsquo;m, I am just goofing everybody up anyway. So, I just wanted to say thanks&hellip; thanks for trying.</p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr"><strong>Mr. Holland</strong>: Is it any fun?</p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr"><strong>Miss Lang</strong>: I wanted it to be.</p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr"><strong>Mr. Holland</strong>: You know what we&rsquo;ve been doing wrong, Miss Lang? We&rsquo;ve been playing the notes on the page.</p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr"><strong>Miss Lang</strong>: Well, what else is there to play?</p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr"><strong>Mr. Holland</strong>: Well&hellip; A lot more to music than notes on a page. These guys for example&hellip; now, they can&rsquo;t sing and they have absolutely no harmonics. They&rsquo;re playing the same three chords over and over again and I love it. Do you?</p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr"><strong>Miss Lang</strong>: Yeah.</p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr"><strong>Mr. Holland</strong>: Yeah. Why?&nbsp;</p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr"><strong>Miss Lang</strong>: I don&rsquo;t know.</p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr"><strong>Mr. Holland</strong>: Yeah you do.</p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr"><strong>Miss Lang</strong>: Because it&rsquo;s fun.</p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr"><strong>Mr. Holland</strong>: That&rsquo;s right. Because playing music is supposed to be fun. It&rsquo;s about heart, it&rsquo;s about feelings and moving people, and something beautiful and being alive and it&rsquo;s not about notes on a page, I could teach you notes on a page, I can&rsquo;t teach you that other stuff. Do me a favor, pick up your clarinet and play with me.</p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr"><strong>Miss Lang</strong>: Okay.</p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr"><strong>Mr. Holland</strong>: And this time, no music.</p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr"><strong>Miss Lang</strong>: Oh&hellip; what&hellip;</p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr"><strong>Mr. Holland</strong>: Because you already know it. It&rsquo;s already in your head and your fingers and your heart, you just don&rsquo;t trust yourself to know that. Okay, here we go, ready? one, two, three, four&hellip;</p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr"><strong>Miss Lang</strong>: Ugh.</p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr"><strong>Mr. Holland</strong>: Okay, let&rsquo;s do it again and this time not so much lip on the mouthpiece.</p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr"><strong>Miss Lang</strong>: Okay.</p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr"><strong>Mr. Holland</strong>: One, two, three, four&hellip;</p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr"><strong>Miss Lang</strong>: Ugh!</p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr"><strong>Mr. Holland</strong>: Alright, no, no, don&rsquo;t do that. Let me ask you a question.</p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr"><strong>Miss Lang</strong>: What?</p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr"><strong>Mr. Holland</strong>: When you look in the mirror what do you like best about yourself?</p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr"><strong>Miss Lang</strong>: My hair.</p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr"><strong>Mr. Holland</strong>: Why?</p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr"><strong>Miss Lang</strong>: My father always says that it reminds him of the sunset.</p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr"><strong>Mr. Holland</strong>: Play the sunset. Close your eyes. One, two, three, four&hellip; Don&rsquo;t stop playing!</p>\\r\\n<p>&nbsp;</p>',
                'glossary' => '<p dir="ltr">1. <strong>Alive</strong> (adjective). <strong>e.g.&nbsp;</strong><em>Music keeps the memory of old times alive.</em></p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">2. <strong>Chords </strong>(noun). <strong>e.g. </strong><em>Some jazz musicians experiment with unusual chord structures.</em></p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">3. <strong>Environment </strong>(noun): the external factors and forces surrounding and affecting an organism, person, or population. <strong>e.g.</strong>&nbsp;<em>She works in a friendly environment with helpful coworkers.</em></p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">4. <strong>Harmonics </strong>(noun): related to sound quality and resonance. <strong>e.g.&nbsp;</strong><em>The teacher explained how harmonics work in different instruments.</em></p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">5. <strong>Instead </strong>(adverb): an alternative, something in place of other. <strong>e.g.</strong>&nbsp;<em>He didn&rsquo;t go to the concert. He watched it online instead.</em></p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">6. <strong>Lose interest in something</strong>: to stop caring about or enjoying something that used to be interesting. <strong>e.g.<em> </em></strong><em>He<strong> </strong>lost interest in guitar after a few months and stopped practicing.</em></p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">7. <strong>Make things worse for others</strong> (expression): to do something that causes more problems or difficulties for other people. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>By arriving late, he made things worse for others<strong> </strong>who had to wait for him.</em></p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">8. <strong>Mirror</strong> (noun): a surface, typically of glass, which reflects a clear image. <strong>e.g.&nbsp;</strong><em>She was singing in front of the mirror to practice her performance.</em></p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">9. <strong>Mouthpiece </strong>(noun).<strong> e.g.</strong> <em>He carefully placed the mouthpiece on his trumpet before starting to play.</em></p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">10. <strong>Move people</strong> (idiom): to affect someone emotionally. <strong>e.g.</strong>&nbsp;<em>Good music can move people deeply, making them feel emotions they didn&rsquo;t expect.</em></p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">11. <strong>Musical expression</strong>: Musical expression is the art of playing or singing with a personal response to the music. <strong>e.g.&nbsp;</strong><em>Musical expression is not just about playing the right notes but also about how you play them.</em></p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">12. <strong>Opus </strong>(noun): a work of art, especially a musical composition, often one that is large, complex, or important. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>The composer&rsquo;s latest opus is a symphony in three movements.</em></p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">13. <strong>Play in tune</strong> (expression):&nbsp; to play music at the correct pitch, so all the notes sound exactly right. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>The band played perfectly in tune, and the audience loved it.</em></p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">14. <strong>Remind someone of</strong> (verb): to make someone think of something or someone else. <strong>e.g.</strong>&nbsp;<em>That song reminds them of their wedding day.</em></p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">15. <strong>Ruin things for others </strong>(idiom):&nbsp; To spoil or damage a situation or activity for other people. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>She was afraid her mistake would ruin the performance for everyone else.</em></p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">16. <strong>Scholarship </strong>(noun). <strong>e.g.</strong>&nbsp;<em>She received a full scholarship to study engineering at a top university.&nbsp;</em></p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">17. <strong>School&rsquo;s out</strong> (expression): school is closed for a break, such as a summer vacation or a holiday.&nbsp;<strong>e.g. </strong><em>As soon as school\'s out, we\'re heading to the beach.</em></p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">18. <strong>Skilled at</strong> (verb): to have the ability or training to do something well, especially after learning and practice.<em> <strong>e.g.</strong> She is highly skilled at playing the piano and performs in concerts regularly.</em></p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">19. <strong>To skip</strong> (verb): to leave one thing or place, especially quickly, in order to go to another. <strong>e.g. </strong><em>They skipped the introduction and went straight to the chorus.</em></p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">20. <strong>Strength</strong> (noun): the quality of being strong, either physically or mentally. A good quality or skill that someone has. <strong>e.g.</strong>&nbsp;<em>Her main strength is staying calm under pressure during exams.</em></p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">21. <strong>Stuff </strong>(noun). <strong>e.g.</strong>&nbsp;<em>Do you have your music stuff with you &mdash; like your guitar and cables?</em></p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">22. <strong>Technical accuracy</strong> (noun): how correctly and precisely someone performs a task. In music it refers to playing the correct notes and rhythm. <strong>e.g.</strong>&nbsp;<em>The pianist played the song with great technical accuracy, and every note was perfect.</em></p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">23. <strong>There is room for </strong>(idiom): It means that something is possible, welcome, or has the potential to exist, be included, or be improved. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>There is room for improvement in your presentation&mdash;you explained the topic well, but you could add more examples.</em></p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">24. <strong>Trust yourself</strong> (verb phrase). <strong>e.g.&nbsp;</strong><em>If you want to succeed, you have to trust yourself and your decisions.</em></p>',
                'translation' => null,
                'dictionary' => '<p><a href="https://dictionary.cambridge.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cambridge Dictionary</a></p>\\r\\n<p>&nbsp;</p>',
                'listening_tips_enabled' => true,
