            [
                'id' => 4000,
                'title' => 'U1: Beats move us',
                'author' => 'We are your friends',
                'description' => '<p>In this unit, you will <strong>complete </strong>several comprehension exercises about <strong>a DJ\'s take on how to get people moving</strong>. You will also <strong>define </strong>your goals, <strong>identify </strong>persistent comprehension problems and <strong>reflect </strong>on strategies to compensate for them.</p>',
                'listening_tips' => '<p>Before listening, ask yourself: <strong>What do I need to understand? </strong>(e.g., the topic, a detail, an opinion). <strong>Focus on</strong> the main idea, not every single word. <strong>Don&rsquo;t panic</strong> if you don&rsquo;t understand everything.</p>',
                'cultural_notes' => '<p>1. <strong>buchla</strong> (noun): it refers to a family of modular synthesizers and related electronic instruments designed by Don Buchla beginning in the mid-1960s. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>The musician experimented with a buchla synthesizer to create unique electronic sounds for his performance.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>2. <strong>club soda</strong> (noun): is a form of carbonated water with added minerals manufactured in North America, commonly used as a drink mixer.&nbsp;<strong>e.g.</strong> <em>He ordered a club soda with lime because he wanted something refreshing without alcohol.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>3. <strong>David Bowie </strong>(noun): he was an English singer-songwriter and actor (1947&ndash;2016) whose innovative music and ever-changing personas made him one of rock&rsquo;s most influential figures.&nbsp;<strong>e.g.</strong> <em>David Bowie</em><em> was known not only for his music but also for constantly reinventing his image.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>4. <strong>Juan Atkins </strong>(noun): is an American musician and producer, widely recognized as one of the founding fathers of techno music.&nbsp;<strong>e.g.</strong>&nbsp;<em>Many electronic music producers today say they were influenced by Juan Atkins&rsquo; early tracks.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>5. <strong>Skrillex</strong> (noun): is a record producer, DJ, musician, singer &amp; songwriter from Los Angeles who has won 8 Grammy Awards, been named MTV&rsquo;s Electronic Dance Music Artist of the Year &amp; holds the record for most Grammys won by an electronic artist.&nbsp;<strong>e.g</strong>. <em>Skrillex gained popularity for his energetic dubstep tracks and bold production style.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>6. <strong>ros&eacute;</strong> (noun): is a type of wine distinguished by its pink color, which comes from the limited skin contact with red grapes during winemaking.&nbsp;<strong>e.g.</strong> <em>We had a glass of chilled ros&eacute; with our lunch on the terrace&mdash;it was perfect for the sunny weather.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>7. <strong>Wurlitzer</strong> (noun): a brand of musical instruments, best known for its electric pianos and jukeboxes.&nbsp;<strong>e.g.</strong> <em>The vintage caf&eacute; had an old Wurlitzer jukebox that still played classic rock songs.</em></p>',
                'transcript' => '<p dir="ltr">1. <strong>Cole:</strong> What\'s in there, Ros&eacute;?&nbsp;</p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">2. <strong>Sophie:</strong> Here.</p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">3. <strong>Cole:</strong> Club soda&nbsp;</p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">4. <strong>Sophie: </strong>With cranberry</p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">5. <strong>Cole:</strong> Very stealth.&nbsp;</p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">6. <strong>Sophie:</strong> Hey, you know this party is looking a little stiff, Cole.</p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">7. <strong>Cole: </strong>So what are you saying? Do I need to amp it up?&nbsp;</p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">8. <strong>Sophie:</strong> You could try, but this crowd doesn\'t dance before midnight.</p>\\r\\n<p>9.<strong> Cole:</strong> Ok. rocking a party. Step one. So it\'s the DJs job to get the crowd out of their heads and into their bodies. So in order to do that, you need at the very least a caveman sense of rhythm, a cursory knowledge of mathematics and the broad strokes of 9th grade biology. For example, the baseline controls this region of the body right here. The most important region. There\'s always going to be resistance, so you&rsquo;ve got to be patient. Look for that one person who\'s not afraid to just go for it. Next, you want to zero in on their heartbeats. I like to start them off at about 120 beats per minute. That\'s equivalent to the heartbeat of a long distance runner. You see, BPM is the name of the game. It governs how your body moves.For example, reggae is slow, about 60 BPM. Dubstep is actually 140 BPM cut to half speed. It ends up being about 70 BPM. House is around 110 to 130 BPM. Then there\'s hardcore. Not sure how you dance that. Once you\'ve locked onto their heart rate, start bringing them up song by song. There&rsquo;s a popular myth that at 128 beats per minute is the rate that synergizes most with your heartbeat. That\'s the magic number. Once you\'ve gotten your crowd there, you\'re controlling their entire circulatory system.</p>',
                'glossary' => '<p>1.<strong> amp</strong> (something) up (phrasal verbs): to make something stronger, more extreme, or more exciting.&nbsp;<strong>e.g.</strong>&nbsp;<em>If you\'re ready to amp up the adventure, you can climb up via the waterfall.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>2.<strong> broad stroke(s)</strong> (idiom): a general outline or simplified version of something. <strong>e.g. </strong>She described the plan in <em>broad strokes</em>, but left the specifics for the next meeting.</p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">3. <strong>caveman</strong> (noun): someone who lived in a cave in the early stages of the development of human society. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>The topic extends from the cavemen to the exploration of other worlds.</em></p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">4. <strong>crowd</strong> (noun). <strong>e.g.</strong>&nbsp;<em>The singer paused to thank the crowd for their support, clearly moved by their enthusiastic response.</em></p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">5. <strong>crush it </strong>(informal phrase).&nbsp;<strong>e.g</strong>. <em>Keep going! You\'re crushing it!</em></p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">6. <strong>cursory </strong>(adjective): quick and probably not detailed. <strong>e.g.</strong>&nbsp;<em>He gave the picture a cursory glance.</em></p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">7. <strong>end up&nbsp;</strong>(phrasal verb): to reach a particular place or achieve a situation after other activities. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>We only wanted to record a demo, but we ended up producing a full-length album that went viral.</em></p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">8.&nbsp;<em><strong>entire </strong>(adjective). <strong>e.g.</strong> The artist gained international fame after his entire album was featured in a popular Netflix series.</em></p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">9. <strong>executive</strong> (adjective): relating to making decisions and managing businesses or government. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>The board held an executive meeting to discuss the company\'s long-term vision and financial strategy.</em></p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">10. <strong>gap year</strong> (noun):&nbsp; a year between leaving school and starting university that is usually spent travelling or working. <strong>e.g. </strong><em>She took a gap year to travel and volunteer abroad before starting university.</em></p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">11. <strong>go for it</strong> (idiom): to do anything you have to in order to get something. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>If you\'re passionate about becoming a DJ, don&rsquo;t hold back &mdash; go for it and start practicing every day.</em></p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">12. <strong>lock on(to) something</strong> (phrasal verb): to focus your attention on a specific element and align your actions with it.<strong>&nbsp;e.g.</strong> <em>The camera can automatically lock on to a moving object to keep it in focus.</em></p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">13. <strong>mild </strong>(adjective):&nbsp; not violent, severe, or extreme. <strong>e.g. </strong><em>The curry was quite mild, so even people who don&rsquo;t like spicy food could enjoy it.</em></p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">14. <strong>networking</strong> (noun): the activity of meeting people who might be useful to know, especially in your job. <strong>e.g.</strong>&nbsp;<em>She attended several conferences to improve her networking skills and meet potential employers.</em></p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">15. <strong>obscure</strong> (adjective): not known to many people. <strong>e.g.&nbsp;</strong><em>The author often uses obscure references that only a few readers might understand.</em></p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">16. <strong>pace</strong> (noun): the speed at which someone or something moves, or with which something happens or changes. <strong>e.g.</strong>&nbsp;<em>He ran at a steady pace throughout the marathon and finished without feeling exhausted.</em></p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">17.<strong> rehearse</strong> (verb): to practise a play, a piece of music, etc. in order to prepare it for public performance. <strong>e.g</strong>. <em>Although the soloist had rehearsed the piece countless times, she still felt a surge of adrenaline before going on stage.</em></p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">18. <strong>rhythm</strong> (noun): a strong pattern of sounds, words, or musical notes that is used in music, poetry, and dancing. <strong>e.g.</strong>&nbsp;<em>Once you find the rhythm of the song, it becomes easier to dance along.</em></p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">19. <strong>start someone off </strong>(phrasal verb): to help a person begin an activity by giving initial guidance, support, or resources. <strong>e.g.</strong>&nbsp;<em>The teacher started us off with a simple exercise to warm up our brains before the main lesson.</em></p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">20. stealth (adjective). e.g.&nbsp;<em>His stealth marketing campaign relied on influencers subtly featuring the track without mentioning it directly.</em></p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">21. <strong>stiff</strong> (adjective). <strong>e.g.&nbsp;</strong><em>The new guitarist seemed stiff during rehearsals, probably because it was her first time playing with a professional band.</em></p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">22. <strong>synergize</strong> (verb). <strong>e.g. </strong><em>The band\'s strength lies in how each section synergizes with the others, creating a rich and balanced sound.</em></p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">23. <strong>thoroughly</strong> (adverb): in a detailed and careful way. <strong>e.g.</strong>&nbsp;<em>The students thoroughly examine their essays before handing them to the teacher. They want an excellent grade.</em></p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">24. <strong>torn</strong> (adjective): finding it difficult to choose between two possibilities. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>He was torn between accepting the job offer and continuing his studies abroad.</em></p>\\r\\n<p dir="ltr">25. <strong>zero in</strong> (phrasal verb). <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>While writing the song, they zeroed in on the theme of resilience, crafting lyrics that resonated deeply with listeners.</em></p>',
