            [
                'id' => 4002,
                'title' => 'U3: Passion for music',
                'author' => 'LalaLand',
                'description' => '<p>In this unit, you will <strong>complete </strong>several comprehension exercises about <strong>two people discussing their take on jazz.</strong> You will also <strong>define </strong>your goals, <strong>identify </strong>persistent comprehension problems and <strong>reflect </strong>on strategies to compensate for them.</p>',
                'listening_tips' => '<p><strong>Notice what&rsquo;s difficult</strong>: speed, new words, unclear pronunciation and <strong>use support tools</strong> wisely &ndash; captions, transcripts, or slower playback. &nbsp;<strong>Use</strong> them as <strong>scaffolds</strong>, not crutches.</p>',
                'cultural_notes' => '<p><strong>cocktail party</strong>: a formal party with alcoholic drinks, usually in the early evening. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>At the cocktail party, guests drank champagne and chatted casually as a jazz trio played in the background.</em></p>\\r\\n<p><strong id="docs-internal-guid-2493bee4-7fff-c79f-b19d-fcd611ddc783">flop house: </strong>a very cheap, run-down place where people can sleep for the night, often with poor living conditions. Usually used by people with little or no money.<strong> e.g. </strong>After losing his job, he spent a few nights in a<strong> flop house</strong> on the edge of the city, trying to get back on his feet.</p>\\r\\n<p><strong id="docs-internal-guid-3aa0f482-7fff-9c2b-0da1-b713b7ba3570">Kenny Clarke</strong>: Kenny &ldquo;Klook&rdquo; Clarke was a foundational architect of modern jazz drumming, moving the beat to the ride cymbal and adding dynamic, improvisational accents. <strong>e.g. </strong><em>The drummer credited Kenny Clarke&rsquo;s ride cymbal technique as the foundation of his own approach to modern jazz.</em></p>\\r\\n<p><em><strong id="docs-internal-guid-72729da0-7fff-320f-0526-33c4b9052a44">Kenny G: </strong></em>is an American smooth jazz saxophonist, composer, and record producer. He is one of the best-selling artists of all time, with global sales totaling more than 75 million records, making him also the best-selling instrumentalist in history.<strong> e.g. </strong><em>My dad used to relax every evening with a glass of wine and a Kenny G album playing softly in the background.</em></p>\\r\\n<p><strong>Sidney Bechet</strong>: a pioneering American jazz musician, best known for his powerful and expressive playing on the soprano saxophone and clarinet. He was one of the first important soloists in jazz history, emerging at the same time as Louis Armstrong. <strong>e.g.<em> </em></strong><em>Sidney Bechet&rsquo;s soaring soprano saxophone solos gave early jazz a bold, unmistakable voice that still inspires musicians today.</em></p>\\r\\n<p><em><strong id="docs-internal-guid-ff72195e-7fff-7476-4345-77ba6e5aa80f">Thelonious Monk</strong>: </em>Thelonious Monk was a pioneering American jazz pianist and composer who helped shape bebop, a fast-paced, improvisation-heavy style that broke from traditional swing. His music is known for its dissonant harmonies, rhythmic complexity, and iconic compositions like "Round Midnight." <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>The pianist studied Thelonious Monk&rsquo;s recordings to better understand the unconventional rhythms and harmonies of bebop.</em></p>',
                'transcript' => '<p><strong>Mia</strong>:<strong> </strong>I should probably tell you something now to get it out of the way. I hate Jazz. Are you okay?&nbsp;</p>\\r\\n<p><strong>Sebastian</strong>:<strong> </strong>What do you mean you hate Jazz?</p>\\r\\n<p><strong>Mia</strong>: Just means that when I listen to it, I don\'t like it.</p>\\r\\n<p><strong>Sebastian</strong>: Yeah, but it\'s such a blank statement you don\'t like jazz. What are you doing right now?&nbsp;</p>\\r\\n<p><strong>Mia</strong>:<strong> </strong>Nothing.&nbsp;</p>\\r\\n<p><strong>Sebastian</strong>: I just think that people, when they say that they, you know, hate jazz. They just&hellip; they don\'t have context. They don\'t know where it comes from. Jazz was born in a little flop house in New Orleans, and it just&hellip; because people were crammed in there, they spoke five different languages. They couldn\'t talk to each other. The only way they could communicate was with jazz.</p>\\r\\n<p><strong>Mia</strong>: What about Kenny G?&nbsp;</p>\\r\\n<p><strong>Sebastian</strong>: What?</p>\\r\\n<p><strong>Mia</strong>: what about Kenny G? I mean, what about elevator music? You know, jazz music that I know.&nbsp;</p>\\r\\n<p><strong>Sebastian</strong>:<strong> </strong>What about it?&nbsp;</p>\\r\\n<p><strong>Mia</strong>:<strong> </strong>From my life I just, I mean, I find it relaxing.&nbsp;</p>\\r\\n<p><strong>Sebastian</strong>:<strong> </strong>It\'s not relaxing. It\'s not, it\'s not. Sidney Bechet shot somebody because they told him he played the wrong note. That\'s hardly relaxing.&nbsp;</p>\\r\\n<p><strong>Mia</strong>: Where I grew up, there was this station called K jazz 103, and people would just put on that station when they had a cocktail party, and everyone would just kind of talk over it.</p>\\r\\n<p><strong>Sebastian</strong>:<strong> </strong>I know that\'s the&hellip; Okay, okay. So I think that that\'s part of the premise that you can\'t hear it, you know. You have to see it. You have to see what\'s at stake. I mean, look at this fellas. Look at the&hellip;Look at the sax player right now. He just hijacked the song. He\'s on his own trip. Every one of these guys is composing, they\'re rearranging, they\'re writing, and they\'re playing the melody. They\'re just&hellip; And now, look at the trumpet player. He\'s got his own idea, and so it\'s conflict and it\'s compromise, and it\'s just, it\'s new every time. It\'s brand new, every night, very, very exciting. And it\'s dying. It\'s dying, Mia, it\'s dying on the vine. And the world says: let it die. It had its time. Well, not on my watch.</p>\\r\\n<p><strong>Mia</strong>: What are you gonna do?&nbsp;</p>\\r\\n<p><strong>Sebastian</strong>: Have my own club.</p>\\r\\n<p><strong>Mia</strong>: Really?&nbsp;</p>\\r\\n<p><strong>Sebastian</strong>: Yes, we\'re gonna play whatever we want, whenever we want, however we want, as long as it\'s pure jazz.</p>',
                'glossary' => '<p>1. <strong>aggravated</strong> (by something or someone): annoyed. <strong>e.g. </strong><em>He was clearly aggravated by his colleague&rsquo;s constant interruptions during the meeting.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>2. <strong>assumption</strong> (noun): something that you accept as true without question or proof<strong>. e.g. </strong><em>They based the entire marketing plan on the assumption that social media engagement would double overnight.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>3. <strong>at stake </strong>(idiom): <strong>e.g.</strong>&nbsp;<em>With millions of dollars at stake, the team worked late into the night to finalize their proposal.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>4. <strong>awe </strong>(noun): a feeling of great respect sometimes mixed with fear or surprise. <strong>e.g.</strong>&nbsp;<em>As children we were rather in awe of our grandfather.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>5. <strong>blank statement</strong> (noun phrase): a broad generalization that may not be accurate or fair to all members of a group. <strong>e.g</strong>.&nbsp;<em>Declaring that introverts hate public speaking is a blank statement that overlooks those who, despite their nature, deliver powerful speeches.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>6. <strong>character </strong>(noun): the particular combination of qualities in someone or something that makes them or it different from others.<strong> e.g. </strong><em>The lead singer has such a strong character that it shapes the band&rsquo;s entire image.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>7. <strong>crammed </strong>(adjective): very full of people or things<strong>. e.g.</strong>&nbsp;<em>By midday the tiny caf&eacute; was crammed with tourists seeking shelter from the rain.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>8. <strong>compromise </strong>(verb): <strong>e.g.</strong>&nbsp;<em>After hours of negotiation, both sides agreed to a compromise that satisfied neither completely but moved the deal forward.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>9. <strong>core </strong>(noun): the basic and most important part of something. <strong>e.g. </strong><em>The lyrics may change, but emotion remains at the core of every great song.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>10. <strong>die on the vine</strong> (idiom): it\'s a metaphorical way of saying that a project, idea or plan has failed&nbsp; before it had a chance to develop or succeed <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>Without enough support from the community, the music festival died on the vine before it even started.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>11. <strong>flawed </strong>(adjective): not perfect, or containing mistakes. <strong>e.g.</strong>&nbsp;<em>Critics said the album was flawed in its production quality, but praised the lyrics and creativity.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>12. <strong>get something out of the way</strong>:<strong>&nbsp;e.g.&nbsp;</strong><em>Let&rsquo;s get this paperwork out of the way to enjoy the rest of our afternoon.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>13. <strong>had its time</strong> (idiom): <strong>e.g</strong>.&nbsp;<em>Vinyl records</em> had their time<em><strong> </strong>in the spotlight, but now most people stream music digitally.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>14. <strong>harvest&nbsp;</strong>(verb): to pick and collect crops, or to collect plants, animals, or fish as food. <strong>e.g.&nbsp;</strong><em>Every autumn, the family gathers to harvest the apples from their orchard before the first frost.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>15. <strong>heated </strong>(adjective): excited or angry. <strong>e.g.<em> </em></strong><em>Their discussion grew so heated that both participants were shouting by the end.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>16. <strong>hijack </strong>(verb): <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>Cyber attackers can hijack your Wi"æFi connection to intercept personal data without your knowledge.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>17. <strong>incidental </strong>(adjective): less important than the thing something is connected with or part of. <strong>e.g.</strong>&nbsp;<em>The documentary focuses on climate change, with only incidental references to politics.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>18. <strong>issue </strong>(noun): a subject or problem that people are thinking and talking about. <strong>e.g.</strong>&nbsp;<em>Climate change is a critical issue that governments around the world are striving to address.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>19. <strong>lighthouse </strong>(noun): a tall building near the coast or shore with a flashing light at the top to warn ships of rocks and other dangers. <strong>e.g.</strong>&nbsp;<em>The lighthouse keeper maintained the light manually before everything became automated.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>20. <strong>means </strong>(noun): a method or way of doing something.<strong> e.g.</strong>&nbsp;<em>We need to find some other means of transportation.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>21. <strong>on someone&rsquo;s watch</strong> (idiom): <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>Never before had a safety violation occurred<strong> </strong>on her watch, so she took the recent accident very personally.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>22. <strong>premise </strong>(noun): <strong>e.g.</strong><em>The novel&rsquo;s premise that dreams can alter reality captivated readers from the first page</em></p>\\r\\n<p>23. <strong>reach </strong>(verb): to succeed in advertising or selling a product or service to a particular group of people. <strong>e.g.</strong>&nbsp;<em>The ad campaign helped the brand reach young professionals through targeted social media ads.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>24. <strong>rearrange </strong>(verb): <strong>e.g.</strong>&nbsp;<em>She decided to<strong> </strong>rearrange her furniture to create a more open and inviting living space.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>25. <strong>soothing </strong>(adjective): making you feel calm. <strong>e.g.</strong> <em>The gentle sound of rainfall through the window was so soothing that he fell asleep instantly.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>26. <strong>take something lightly</strong> (idiom): To not treat something as serious or important enough; to underestimate the seriousness of a situation. <strong>e.g. </strong><em>The band<strong> </strong>took the soundcheck lightly and ended up having technical issues during the show.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>27. <strong>trend </strong>(noun): a general development or change in a situation or in the way that people are behaving.<strong> e.g. </strong><em>There&rsquo;s a growing trend toward plant"æbased diets among health"æconscious consumers.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>28. <strong>underlying </strong>(adjective): real but not immediately obvious. <strong>e.g.</strong>&nbsp;<em>The underlying cause of the software crash turned out to be a simple typo in the code.</em></p>',
